e: \n\n2002\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n144\n\nFormat: \n\nPaperback\n\nPrice: \n\n9.95\n\nISBN: \n\n0486425436\n\nCategory: \n\nGeneral\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nAllen Stenger\n\n, on\n\n05/27/2015\n\n]\n\nThis is a dated survey of algebraic structures. Despite the title, it does not cover all of mathematics, but just abstract algebra. The skeleton key is an analogy and is explained more clearly in the body as a master key: abstraction in mathematics gives us a way to unlock many problems, just as a master key allows us to unlock many locks, by embodying what they have in common. The present work is a Dover 2002 unaltered reprint of the 1949 Hutchinson & Company edition.\n\nTypically each chapter covers one structure, such as Groups. The chapter gives a strong motivation for the structure (this is the best part of the book), defines it, gives some examples, and outlines a few results. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories - Dudley Ernest Littlewood - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=q6wu-UpwhK4C)\nciteturn982search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; # The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic TheoriesDudley Ernest Littlewood Courier Corporation, Jan 1, 2002 - Mathematics - 138 pages A straightforward explanation of the abstract principles common to science and mathematics, this helpful volume enables scientists and mathematicians to read and appreciate papers in fields different from their own. ... Snippet view - 1949 ... Dover books on mathematics ... ISBN  | 0486425436, 9780486425436\n\n# The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\n\nDudley Ernest LittlewoodCourier Corporation, Jan 1, 2002 - Mathematics - 138 pages A straightforward explanation of the abstract principles common to science and mathemati